      <a href="unit7-lesson1.html" class="sidebar-link active"><div class="sidebar-dot"></div> L1: Logic and Propositions</a>
      <div class="sidebar-link" style="opacity:.4;cursor:not-allowed"><div class="sidebar-dot"></div> L2: Methods of Proof 🔒</div>
    </div>
  </aside>

  <main class="lesson-main">

    <div class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="../../../../../entrance-exam/index.html">Entrance Exam</a> ›
      <a href="../../index.html">Grade 12</a> ›
      <a href="../index.html">Mathematics</a> ›
      Unit 7 · Lesson 1
    </div>

    <div class="lesson-header">
      <div class="lesson-unit-tag">Unit 7 · Mathematical Proofs</div>
      <h1>🧠 Logic and Propositions</h1>
      <p>The language of mathematics is logic. Before you can write or understand a proof, you must master propositions, logical connectives, truth tables and the key logical equivalences that underpin all mathematical reasoning.</p>
      <div class="lesson-meta">
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">📖 <strong>Reading:</strong> ~18 min</div>
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">✏️ <strong>Examples:</strong> 5 worked</div>
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">🎯 <strong>Quiz:</strong> 5 questions</div>
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">🎥 <strong>Videos:</strong> 4 resources</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
    <div class="section-block" id="intro">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">🎯</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Introduction</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">What you will learn in this lesson</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="content-box">
        <p style="font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem">
          Every mathematical theorem, proof and argument is built from <strong>propositions</strong> — statements that are either true or false. Logic gives us the rules for combining propositions and reasoning correctly from one to another. Without logic, proofs collapse into opinions.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--text)">By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
        <ul style="margin-top:.75rem;padding-left:1.25rem;font-size:.9rem;line-height:2;color:var(--text)">
          <li>Define a <strong>proposition</strong> and determine its truth value</li>
          <li>Apply the five logical connectives: negation, conjunction, disjunction, implication, biconditional</li>
          <li>Construct and complete <strong>truth tables</strong> for compound propositions</li>
          <li>Identify <strong>tautologies</strong> (always true) and <strong>contradictions</strong> (always false)</li>
          <li>State the <strong>converse, inverse and contrapositive</strong> of a conditional statement</li>
          <li>Apply <strong>De Morgan's Laws</strong> to simplify negations</li>
          <li>Recognise logically equivalent statements</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- EXPLANATION -->
    <div class="section-block" id="explanation">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">📖</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Explanation</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">Complete theory with visual aids</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- 2.1 Propositions -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.1 Propositions and Truth Values</h3>

        <div class="definition-box">
          <div class="def-label">📌 Proposition</div>
          <p>A <strong>proposition</strong> (or statement) is a declarative sentence that is either <strong>True (T)</strong> or <strong>False (F)</strong> — never both and never neither.<br><br>
          Propositions are usually labelled with letters: p, q, r, s.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:1rem;margin-top:.875rem">
          <div style="background:#F0FDF4;border:1px solid #BBF7D0;border-radius:var(--radius-lg);padding:1rem">
            <div style="font-size:.78rem;font-weight:700;color:#166534;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.5rem">✓ ARE propositions</div>
            <ul style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.9;padding-left:1rem">
              <li>"2 + 2 = 4" (True)</li>
              <li>"Ethiopia is in Europe" (False)</li>
              <li>"7 is a prime number" (True)</li>
              <li>"All cats have wings" (False)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div style="background:#FEF2F2;border:1px solid #FECACA;border-radius:var(--radius-lg);padding:1rem">
            <div style="font-size:.78rem;font-weight:700;color:#991B1B;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.5rem">✗ NOT propositions</div>
            <ul style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.9;padding-left:1rem">
              <li>"What time is it?" (question)</li>
              <li>"Close the door." (command)</li>
              <li>"x + 1 = 5" (open sentence — depends on x)</li>
              <li>"This statement is false." (paradox)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- 2.2 Logical connectives -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.2 The Five Logical Connectives</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:.875rem">These connectives combine simple propositions into <strong>compound propositions</strong>:</p>

        <div class="connective-grid">
          <div class="connective-card">
            <div class="connective-symbol">¬p</div>
            <div class="connective-name">Negation</div>
            <div class="connective-read">Read: "not p"</div>
            <div class="connective-rule">True when p is False. False when p is True. Flips the truth value.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="connective-card">
            <div class="connective-symbol">p ∧ q</div>
            <div class="connective-name">Conjunction</div>
            <div class="connective-read">Read: "p and q"</div>
            <div class="connective-rule">True ONLY when BOTH p and q are True. False otherwise.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="connective-card">
            <div class="connective-symbol">p ∨ q</div>
            <div class="connective-name">Disjunction</div>
            <div class="connective-read">Read: "p or q"</div>
            <div class="connective-rule">False ONLY when BOTH p and q are False. True otherwise (inclusive or).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="connective-card">
            <div class="connective-symbol">p → q</div>
            <div class="connective-name">Implication</div>
            <div class="connective-read">Read: "if p then q"</div>
            <div class="connective-rule">False ONLY when p is True and q is False. True in all other cases.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="connective-card">
            <div class="connective-symbol">p ↔ q</div>
            <div class="connective-name">Biconditional</div>
            <div class="connective-read">Read: "p if and only if q"</div>
            <div class="connective-rule">True when p and q have the SAME truth value. False when they differ.</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="note-box" style="margin-top:1rem">
          <div class="note-label">⚠ The tricky one — Implication (p → q)</div>
          <p>The implication p → q is <strong>only false when the hypothesis (p) is true but the conclusion (q) is false</strong>. When p is false, the implication is automatically true — called "vacuously true." Example: "If it rains, the ground is wet." If it does NOT rain, the statement cannot be falsified regardless of the ground.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- 2.3 Truth tables -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.3 Truth Tables</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:.875rem">
          A truth table lists every possible combination of truth values for the component propositions and the resulting truth value of the compound proposition. For n propositions there are 2ⁿ rows.
        </p>

        <p style="font-size:.875rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.5rem">All five connectives — summary table:</p>
        <table class="truth-table">
          <thead><tr><th>p</th><th>q</th><th>¬p</th><th>p ∧ q</th><th>p ∨ q</th><th>p → q</th><th>p ↔ q</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="T">T</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="F">F</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:1rem;margin-top:1rem">
          <div class="definition-box" style="margin-bottom:0">
            <div class="def-label">📌 Tautology</div>
            <p>A compound proposition that is <strong>always True</strong> regardless of the truth values of its components.<br>Example: p ∨ ¬p is always True ("the law of excluded middle").</p>
          </div>
          <div class="definition-box" style="margin-bottom:0">
            <div class="def-label">📌 Contradiction</div>
            <p>A compound proposition that is <strong>always False</strong> regardless of the truth values of its components.<br>Example: p ∧ ¬p is always False.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- 2.4 Conditional and related -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.4 Conditional Statement and Its Relatives</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:.875rem">
          Given the conditional <strong>p → q</strong> ("if p then q"), three related statements can be formed:
        </p>

        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.875rem">
          <thead>
            <tr style="background:var(--brand-primary);color:#fff">
              <th style="padding:.6rem .875rem;text-align:left">Statement</th>
              <th style="padding:.6rem .875rem;text-align:left">Form</th>
              <th style="padding:.6rem .875rem;text-align:left">Logically equivalent to</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="background:var(--brand-light)">
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);font-weight:700">Conditional (original)</td>
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);font-family:Georgia,serif">p → q</td>
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)">Contrapositive</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);font-weight:700">Converse</td>
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);font-family:Georgia,serif">q → p</td>
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)">Inverse</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="background:var(--surface-2)">
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);font-weight:700">Inverse</td>
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);font-family:Georgia,serif">¬p → ¬q</td>
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)">Converse</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem;font-weight:700">Contrapositive</td>
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem;font-family:Georgia,serif">¬q → ¬p</td>
              <td style="padding:.6rem .875rem">Conditional (original)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="note-box" style="margin-top:1rem">
          <div class="note-label">📝 Key fact for proofs</div>
          <p>The <strong>conditional and its contrapositive are logically equivalent</strong> — they always have the same truth value. This is why proving the contrapositive (¬q → ¬p) is a valid way to prove the original statement (p → q). The converse and inverse are equivalent to each other but NOT to the original.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- 2.5 De Morgan's Laws -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.5 De Morgan's Laws</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:.875rem">
          De Morgan's Laws describe how negation distributes over conjunction and disjunction. They are essential for simplifying logical expressions and writing proof by contradiction.
        </p>

        <div class="definition-box">
          <div class="def-label">📌 De Morgan's Laws</div>
          <p>
            <strong style="font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:1.05rem">¬(p ∧ q) ≡ ¬p ∨ ¬q</strong><br>
            "Not (p and q)" is the same as "not p or not q"<br><br>
            <strong style="font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:1.05rem">¬(p ∨ q) ≡ ¬p ∧ ¬q</strong><br>
            "Not (p or q)" is the same as "not p and not q"<br><br>
            <strong>Memory trick:</strong> When negation enters a bracket, the connective flips (∧ ↔ ∨) and both propositions are negated.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background:var(--surface-2);border-radius:var(--radius-lg);padding:1.25rem;margin-top:.875rem">
          <div style="font-size:.875rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.75rem">Real-world example of De Morgan's Law</div>
          <div style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.8">
            <strong>Statement:</strong> "It is NOT the case that (it is raining AND it is cold)"<br>
            <strong>By De Morgan:</strong> "It is NOT raining OR it is NOT cold"<br><br>
            These two phrasings say exactly the same thing — they are logically equivalent.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <!-- FORMULA REFERENCE -->
    <div class="section-block" id="formula">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">📋</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Formula Reference Card</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">Key rules — memorize these for the exam</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="formula-card">
        <div class="formula-card-title">🧠 Lesson 1 — Logic and Propositions</div>
        <div class="formula-grid">
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Negation</div>
            <div class="formula-math">¬p: flips T↔F</div>
            <div class="formula-note">True when p is False; False when p is True</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Conjunction</div>
            <div class="formula-math">p ∧ q: T only if both T</div>
            <div class="formula-note">False if either p or q is False</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Disjunction</div>
            <div class="formula-math">p ∨ q: F only if both F</div>
            <div class="formula-note">True if at least one of p, q is True</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Implication</div>
            <div class="formula-math">p → q: F only if p=T, q=F</div>
            <div class="formula-note">True when p is False (vacuously true)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Biconditional</div>
            <div class="formula-math">p ↔ q: T when p=q</div>
            <div class="formula-note">True iff both same truth value</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Contrapositive</div>
            <div class="formula-math">p→q ≡ ¬q→¬p</div>
            <div class="formula-note">Always logically equivalent to original</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">De Morgan 1</div>
            <div class="formula-math">¬(p∧q) ≡ ¬p∨¬q</div>
            <div class="formula-note">Negate a conjunction → disjunction of negations</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">De Morgan 2</div>
            <div class="formula-math">¬(p∨q) ≡ ¬p∧¬q</div>
            <div class="formula-note">Negate a disjunction → conjunction of negations</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- WORKED EXAMPLES -->
    <div class="section-block" id="examples">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">✏️</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Worked Examples</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">5 exam-style problems solved step by step</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header"><span class="example-num">Example 1</span><span class="example-title">Identifying Propositions and Truth Values</span></div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem"><strong>Problem:</strong> Classify each as a proposition or not, and if so give its truth value.<br>
            (a) "5 is an even number" &nbsp; (b) "Is 7 prime?" &nbsp; (c) "x > 3" &nbsp; (d) "The sum of angles in a triangle is 180°"</div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">(a)</span>
            <div class="step-text"><strong>Proposition.</strong> It is a declarative sentence. Truth value: <span class="math">False</span> (5 is odd).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">(b)</span>
            <div class="step-text"><strong>Not a proposition.</strong> It is a question — cannot be True or False.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">(c)</span>
            <div class="step-text"><strong>Not a proposition</strong> (as written). It is an open sentence — its truth value depends on x. It becomes a proposition only when x is assigned a value.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">(d)</span>
            <div class="step-text"><strong>Proposition.</strong> Truth value: <span class="math">True</span>.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ (a) Proposition — False &nbsp;|&nbsp; (b) Not a proposition &nbsp;|&nbsp; (c) Not a proposition &nbsp;|&nbsp; (d) Proposition — True</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header"><span class="example-num">Example 2</span><span class="example-title">Constructing a Truth Table</span></div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem"><strong>Problem:</strong> Construct the truth table for (p ∧ q) → ¬p.</div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">Setup</span>
            <div class="step-text">Two propositions → 4 rows. Columns needed: p, q, p∧q, ¬p, then (p∧q)→¬p.</div>
          </div>
          <table class="truth-table">
            <thead><tr><th>p</th><th>q</th><th>p ∧ q</th><th>¬p</th><th>(p ∧ q) → ¬p</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="T">T</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">Row 1</span>
            <div class="step-text">p=T, q=T → p∧q=T, ¬p=F → T→F = <span class="math">F</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">Rows 2–4</span>
            <div class="step-text">p∧q=F in all → F→anything = <span class="math">T</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ The compound proposition is False only when p=T and q=T.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header"><span class="example-num">Example 3</span><span class="example-title">Converse, Inverse and Contrapositive</span></div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem"><strong>Problem:</strong> Given "If a number is divisible by 4, then it is divisible by 2" (p → q), write the converse, inverse and contrapositive.</div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">Identify</span>
            <div class="step-text">p: "a number is divisible by 4"<br>q: "a number is divisible by 2"</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">Converse</span>
            <div class="step-text">q → p: <span class="math">"If a number is divisible by 2, then it is divisible by 4."</span><br>Truth value: <strong>False</strong> (e.g., 6 is divisible by 2 but not 4).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">Inverse</span>
            <div class="step-text">¬p → ¬q: <span class="math">"If a number is NOT divisible by 4, then it is NOT divisible by 2."</span><br>Truth value: <strong>False</strong> (same as converse — they are equivalent).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">Contrapositive</span>
            <div class="step-text">¬q → ¬p: <span class="math">"If a number is NOT divisible by 2, then it is NOT divisible by 4."</span><br>Truth value: <strong>True</strong> (same as original — they are equivalent).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ Contrapositive is always equivalent to the original. Converse and inverse are equivalent to each other.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header"><span class="example-num">Example 4</span><span class="example-title">Applying De Morgan's Laws</span></div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem"><strong>Problem:</strong> Write the negation of each statement using De Morgan's Laws.<br>
            (a) "It is raining and the road is slippery."<br>
            (b) "The number is positive or it is even."</div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">(a)</span>
            <div class="step-text">Let p = "it is raining", q = "road is slippery". Statement: p ∧ q.<br>
              Negation: ¬(p ∧ q) ≡ ¬p ∨ ¬q<br>
              <span class="math">"It is NOT raining OR the road is NOT slippery."</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">(b)</span>
            <div class="step-text">Let p = "number is positive", q = "number is even". Statement: p ∨ q.<br>
              Negation: ¬(p ∨ q) ≡ ¬p ∧ ¬q<br>
              <span class="math">"The number is NOT positive AND it is NOT even."</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ (a) "Not raining OR road not slippery" &nbsp;|&nbsp; (b) "Not positive AND not even"</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header"><span class="example-num">Example 5</span><span class="example-title">Identifying a Tautology</span></div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem"><strong>Problem:</strong> Show that p → (p ∨ q) is a tautology.</div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">Truth table</span>
            <div class="step-text">Build the table:</div>
          </div>
          <table class="truth-table">
            <thead><tr><th>p</th><th>q</th><th>p ∨ q</th><th>p → (p ∨ q)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="highlight"><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td></tr>
              <tr class="highlight"><td class="T">T</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td></tr>
              <tr class="highlight"><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td><td class="T">T</td></tr>
              <tr class="highlight"><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="F">F</td><td class="T">T</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">Conclusion</span>
            <div class="step-text">The final column is True in all four rows — the statement is a <strong>tautology</strong>. This makes logical sense: if p is true, then surely "p or q" is also true.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ p → (p ∨ q) is a <strong>tautology</strong> — True in all cases.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <!-- QUIZ -->
    <div class="section-block" id="quiz">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">🎯</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Quiz</div>
